From: MahanHD <mahanheidari2003@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] process_pipeline: measure white balance on the preview as it is

process_aaa() reads back the processed preview, which the colour matrix
has already taken to sRGB, and then had libmegapixels multiply the
averages by the DCP's ColorMatrix1. That matrix maps XYZ to camera RGB,
so with a real profile a white preview measured as roughly the camera's
raw neutral, (0.57, 1, 0.59) on the Xperia Z1 Compact, and auto white
balance raised red and blue by about 1.7x every step until they hit the
limit. With the default identity calibration this never showed. Use the
identity for the statistics.

---
--- a/src/process_pipeline.c	2026-10-08 23:10:22.610525643 +0400
+++ b/src/process_pipeline.c	2026-10-08 23:10:22.622360507 +0400
@@ -555,9 +555,12 @@
         uint32_t *center = g_malloc_n(width * height * sizeof(uint32_t), 1);
         glReadPixels(0, height, width, height, GL_RGBA, GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE, center);
 
-        libmegapixels_aaa_set_matrix(&state_proc.stats,
-                                     state_proc.calibration.color_matrix_1,
-                                     state_proc.calibration.color_matrix_2);
+        /*
+         * The statistics come from the processed preview, which the
+         * calibration has already taken to sRGB. The DCP colour matrix maps
+         * XYZ to camera RGB and does not apply here.
+         */
+        libmegapixels_aaa_init(&state_proc.stats);
         libmegapixels_aaa_software_statistics(
                 &state_proc.stats, center, width, height);
 
